
  [34mexport[39m [34mconst[39m preferences = [33mcreatePreferences[39m();

  The factory exists for one reason: injecting a fake [38;5;153mstorage[39m is what makes this testable in plain Node with no browser
  and no DOM shim. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports the [38;5;153mpreferences[39m instance and never calls the factory.

  The surface is deliberately four methods and one flag:

  ┌───────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │      Member       │                                          Behavior                                           │
  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mget(key, [39m         │ Returns the stored value, or [38;5;153mfallback[39m (default [38;5;153mundefined[39m) if unset or unreadable. Never     │
  │ [38;5;153mfallback)[39m         │ throws.                                                                                     │
  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mset(key, value)[39m   │ Stores the value. Returns [38;5;153mtrue[39m if it was durably written, [38;5;153mfalse[39m if it was only held in      │
  │                   │ memory.                                                                                     │
  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mremove(key)[39m       │ Deletes the key. Silent no-op if absent.                                                    │
  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mkeys()[39m            │ Returns the preference names currently stored, without the namespace prefix.                │
  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153misPersistent[39m      │ [38;5;153mfalse[39m when the module fell back to in-memory storage, so a caller can warn the user.        │
  └───────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  [38;5;153mfallback[39m on [38;5;153mget[39m is how you get defaults without the registry that approach C wanted — the caller states the default at
  the call site, and no central list of keys has to exist.

  I'm deliberately [1mnot[0m including three things: a [38;5;153msubscribe[39m/change-event API (approach A reads straight through to
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, so there's no cached state to invalidate and nothing is stale — this only becomes necessary if a
  consumer needs to react, and none exists), a [38;5;153mclear()[39m (it's [38;5;153mkeys().forEach(remove)[39m if you ever need it), and any
  defaults registry. All three are speculative until something calls them.

  Does that interface look right before I move on to the storage layout and failure behavior?

